Modeling changes of the Gatekeeper TA of linaro-swg/kmgk (gatekeeper/ta: ta_gatekeeper.h,
failure_record.h, failure_record.c and gatekeeper_ta.c as published, concatenated in this order),
applied before tools/lang-preprocess.py preprocesses it.  Everything the model leaves out or changes
is here, as C; the [ta.gatekeeper] table of the analysis files only says what to preprocess.

1. A byte array of the model is one char object that holds its whole content (a buffer): the
   signature of a password handle and the HMAC of an auth token are uint8_t buffers, and the structs
   are not __packed (the model has no layout).
2. The failure record table (a static struct of failure_record_t records and a size) is one static
   array per field of a record and a size, as the model has no arrays of structs: GetFailureRecord
   and WriteFailureRecord read and write the fields of a record in the three arrays.  The table has
   two records instead of 32: the scenarios have one user.  The table is a global of the TA, so the
   model keeps it from one command to the next as the instance memory of the TA, and loses it with
   the instance, as upstream does.  ~0ULL (no oldest timestamp yet) is UINT64_MAX.
3. ComputeRetryTimeout: FAILURE_TIMEOUT_MS and DAY_IN_MS are constants of the model (static const
   locals upstream), and FAILURE_TIMEOUT_MS << n is ShiftLeft, a loop, as the model has no shift.
4. The master key: the file name of the secret object is SECRET_ID, a constant of the model, in a
   buffer (secret_ID[] upstream); the key data are one buffer, and one attribute is populated.  A
   short read is not checked (the model has no sizes).
5. Signatures: the HMAC goes to one buffer, which is the signature (no truncation to the size of
   the signature).  The signed message of a password handle is the password: upstream signs the
   salt, the metadata of the handle and the password, concatenated, which the model cannot build
   (it has no byte concatenation).  So two handles of the same password have the same signature
   whatever their salt.  memcmp of two signatures is the equality of the buffers.  An empty
   password is not refused by TA_DoVerify (the model has no lengths).
6. Requests and responses: the HAL passes a request struct and a response struct (gk_*_request_t,
   gk_*_response_t, the fields that upstream serializes, in the same order) instead of the
   serialized buffers, so the deserialization, the checks of the buffer sizes and the serialization
   go.  An absent current password handle (length 0 upstream) is NULL.  TA_InvokeCommandEntryPoint,
   the entry point of the model, passes the request (params[0]) and the response (params[1]); its
   unreachable end, after the switch, goes.
7. The lengths of passwords, messages and signatures are not parameters (the model has no sizes).

The entry points TA_CreateEntryPoint (which creates the secret object when it is missing) and the
session entry points are not modeled: the secret object is in the storage of the TA in the initial
state of a scenario.

